What Really Happens When a Pod Fails in Kubernetes -
Kubernetes is known for its self-healing nature, but what actually happens behind the scenes when a Pod fails?

💥 1️⃣ Pod Failure → A Pod can fail due to a container crash, node failure, or eviction (OOM, disk pressure, etc.).
⚙️ 2️⃣ Kubelet Reaction → The Kubelet restarts the failed container based on its restartPolicy (Always, OnFailure, or Never).
Most Pods in Deployments use Always, so they restart automatically. 
🧩 3️⃣ Controller Action
If the Pod or node is lost —
ReplicaSet or Deployment creates a new Pod to maintain replica count.
StatefulSet recreates the same Pod with its PersistentVolume.
DaemonSet ensures the Pod runs on all nodes.
🧠 4️⃣ Node Failure Handling
If a node goes down:
Node Controller marks it NotReady.
The Scheduler then assigns those Pods to available nodes and restore the cluster’s desired state.

✅ Result: Self-Healing
Your app keeps running — no manual recovery needed.
That’s Kubernetes’ true power: desired state + reconciliation loop = resilience.
